===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|misseltoe}}, {{l|en|misletoe}}, {{l|en|miseltoe}}, {{l|en|misleto}}
* {{l|en|misselden}}, {{l|en|misseldine}}, {{l|en|miseleden}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{m|enm|*mistelto}}, alteration (due to the final ''-n'' being mistaken for the old plural ending) of {{m|enm|*mistelton}}, from {{etyl|ang|en}} {{m|ang|misteltān}}, {{m|ang|mistiltān||mistletoe}}, from {{m|ang|mistel||birdlime, mistletoe, basil}} + {{m|ang|tān||twig, sprig}}, equivalent to {{compound|mistle|tan|lang=en}}. Cognate with Danish {{m|da|mistelten}}, Icelandic {{m|is|mistilteinn}}. More at {{l|en|mistle}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈmɪsəltəʊ/|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# Any of several [[hemiparasitic]] [[evergreen]] plants with white [[berry|berries]] that grow in the crowns of [[oak]]s, apple trees and other trees.
#* {{quote-journal|author=Olivia Judson|authorlink=Olivia Judson|title=The hemiparasite season &#91;print version: Under the hemiparasite, ''International New York Times'', 24–25 December 2014, p.&nbsp;7&#93;|url=http://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/24/opinion/the-hemiparasite-season.html|work={{w|The New York Times}}|date=23 December 2014|passage=[T]he stickiness [of mistletoe berries] persists through the gut, making it hard to defecate '''mistletoe''' seeds with any grace. To deal with this, birds like the [[w:silky-flycatcher|silky flycatcher]], Phainopepla nitens, that are '''mistletoe''' specialists, have evolved a "waggle dance"&nbsp;– also known as butt-wiping behavior&nbsp;– where they clean up by rubbing themselves against twigs. Indeed, the association with bird droppings may even be the origin of the word: Some evidence suggests that "'''mistletoe'''" comes from Anglo-Saxon for "dung on a twig."}}
# A [[sprig]] of these plants used as a [[Christmas]] [[decoration]].
#* {{quote-journal|author=Olivia Judson|authorlink=Olivia Judson|title=The hemiparasite season &#91;print version: Under the hemiparasite, ''International New York Times'', 24–25 December 2014, p.&nbsp;7&#93;|url=http://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/24/opinion/the-hemiparasite-season.html|work={{w|The New York Times}}|date=23 December 2014|passage='''Mistletoe'''. The word conjures winter holidays, office parties, stolen kisses, romance. But appreciation of the plant is no modern thing. Kissing under the '''mistletoe''' has been happening for at least 200 years. And some 2,000 years ago, the druids in which is now Britain venerated the plant when it grew on an oak. When they found it, they dressed in white, harvested it with a golden sickle, and sacrificed two white bulls. Or so says that great Roman, {{w|Pliny the Elder}}, in his "[[w:Natural History (Pliny)|Natural History]]."}}

====Usage notes====
The term '''mistletoe''' is used for both {{taxlink|Viscum album|species|noshow=1}} ([[European mistletoe]]) and {{taxlink|Phoradendron leucarpum|species|noshow=1}} ({{vern|eastern mistletoe}}).

